@prefix cd: <https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/ontology/> .
@prefix ex: <https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/resource/> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dct: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-storyline> {
  ex:rev-functions a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:cumulative-distribution-function ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-functions-h, ex:rev-functions-c, ex:rev-functions-v ; cd:narration "These three pipette questions use one model, not three different datasets. The boxes give the analytical purpose and the diagram gives the mapping. PDF: horizontal value and vertical density; probabilities are areas. CDF: horizontal threshold and vertical cumulative probability, accumulated from the PDF. Inverse CDF: horizontal probability and vertical quantile value. The sketch uses dimensionless Uniform(0,1); the volume mapping later restores mL. Equal numerical coordinates in that sketch are special, not equal meanings for the axes. Bounds 9.98 to 10.02 mL and equal plausibility are assumed uncertainty inputs, not guaranteed manufacturer coverage."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-functions-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "One pipette model: ranges, probabilities and quantiles"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-functions-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-functions-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-functions-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-functions-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-functions-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-functions-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:rev-df-start a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:rev-df ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-df-start-h, ex:rev-df-start-v ; cd:narration "Before chi-square, t and F, introduce degrees of freedom with the actual repeatability data we will reuse. Five independent raw observations are 9.6, 9.8, 10.0, 10.2 and 10.4 mg/L. After fitting mean 10.0, the residuals must sum to zero. Once four residuals are specified, the fifth is forced. Five residuals therefore span four independent directions, although the original observations were independent. We have used one fitted mean. Under normal sampling an orthogonal change of coordinates expresses those directions as independent normal coordinates; the raw centered residuals themselves are not independent. Squaring and adding those independent coordinates is the source of the chi-square reference. This is not an instruction to delete an observation."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-df-start-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Degrees of freedom: fitting a mean leaves one constraint"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-df-start-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-df-start-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-df-start-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-df-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-df-map a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:rev-df ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-df-map-h, ex:rev-df-map-c ; cd:narration "Use constraints, not a memorized universal n-minus-one rule. The five-result variance and t reference each use four residual degrees of freedom. Two independent six-result methods each estimate a mean, giving two ordered counts of five for F. Pearson's later grouped-count example has a fixed total across five categories, so four counts determine the fifth; it has four degrees of freedom, not ninety-nine. Fitted categorical model parameters can consume further independent directions only under a justified fitting scheme. Degrees of freedom identify the appropriate distribution within a family; the sampling assumptions still determine whether that family is valid."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-df-map-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Count the independent directions for each question"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-df-map-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-df-map-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-df-map-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-df-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:rev-chi-process a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-chi-process-h, ex:rev-chi-process-v ; cd:narration "Start with the analytical process: repeat the measurement of one unchanged homogeneous nitrate control under stable conditions. Each new group of five independent normal results gives its own fitted mean and variance. Chi-square describes how that variance estimate fluctuates, after scaling by the fixed true variance. It is not a distribution of concentration values. We will use exactly one constructed five-result series throughout the next slides: calculate its variance, connect that calculation to the reference law, then quantify uncertainty in true repeatability variance. This last step is a confidence interval for variance, not an unexplained interval of concentrations."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-process-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Chi-square process: repeated series give different variances"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-process-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-chi-process-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-process-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-chi-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-calc a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-chi-calc-h, ex:rev-chi-calc-v, ex:rev-chi-calc-c, ex:rev-chi-calc-f ; cd:narration "Here are the actual five teaching values, not a new dataset. The mean is 10.0 mg/L. Their deviations are minus 0.4, minus 0.2, zero, plus 0.2 and plus 0.4 mg/L; the squared deviations sum to 0.40 squared mg/L. Divide by four residual degrees of freedom to obtain sample variance 0.10 squared mg/L, and take its positive root for SD about 0.31623 mg/L. These are observed estimates. Repeating the five-result study would change them. We now ask how the random variance estimate relates to the unknown fixed true method variance. Neither symmetry of these constructed values nor their number proves normality."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-calc-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Our five repeats: calculate the repeatability variance"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-calc-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-chi-calc-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-calc-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-s15a-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-calc-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-chi-data ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-calc-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-s15a-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-reference a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-chi-reference-h, ex:rev-chi-reference-v, ex:rev-chi-reference-c, ex:rev-chi-reference-f ; cd:narration "The same squared-deviation sum is four times the sample variance. Divide by the unknown positive true variance to remove units. Under independent identically normal sampling, this scaled quantity follows chi-square with four degrees of freedom. The reason is the residual constraint introduced earlier: after fitting the mean, the sum of squared standardized residuals can be expressed as the sum of squares of four independent normal coordinates. Its nonnegativity and right skew now have a construction, not just a curve label. True variance is fixed; sample variance and the scaled statistic change across new series. The true variance is unknown in practice, so this identity is not yet a directly evaluated number. Inverting its central probability statement is what produces a confidence interval for true variance on the next slide."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-reference-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Why our variance estimate has a chi-square reference"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-reference-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-chi-reference-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-reference-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16b-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-reference-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-chi-pivot-info ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-reference-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-chi-pivot ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-report a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-chi-report-h, ex:rev-chi-report-c, ex:rev-chi-report-f ; cd:narration "Finish the same repeatability study, not a different example. Observed squared deviations sum to 0.40 and observed variance is 0.10 in squared concentration units. A chi-square-four variable lies between its 0.025 and 0.975 quantiles with probability 0.95. Those quantiles are 0.4844186 and 11.1432868. Solve that inequality for true variance: divide 0.40 by the larger quantile for the lower bound and by the smaller for the upper. The resulting 95 percent confidence interval is 0.03590 to 0.82573 (mg/L) squared. Its width explains the analytical consequence: five repeats give an imprecise variance estimate. The procedure has 95 percent coverage under independent normal repeated sampling; it does not assign a posterior probability to a fixed variance. It is not an interval of individual concentrations and does not include bias or environmental sampling variation."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-report-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Report how uncertain our repeatability variance is"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-report-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-chi-report-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-report-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-chi-interval-info ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-chi-report-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-chi-interval ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:rev-t-process a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-t-process-h, ex:rev-t-process-v ; cd:narration "Now change the analytical question but keep the same five-repeat study. We want the common analytical mean, not the variance itself. Imagine repeating independent normal five-result series. Each gives a different mean and sample SD. The sample mean is normal. If we center it and divide by its known true standard error we obtain standard normal. In practice we estimate that denominator from the same series, so its uncertainty must enter the reference: this standardized ratio is Student-t with four residual degrees of freedom. Do not say that small-sample means themselves become t-distributed. We will compare the two denominator choices, show how increasing df reduces their difference and then calculate the confidence interval for our observed mean."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-process-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Student-t process: estimate a mean and its uncertainty"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-process-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-process-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-process-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-normal a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-t-normal-h, ex:rev-t-normal-v, ex:rev-t-normal-c, ex:rev-t-normal-f ; cd:narration "Under the normal sampling model, the mean is normal for every sample size. Its SD is the individual population SD divided by square root of n. Center at the fixed population mean and divide by that known standard error: the statistic is exactly standard normal. This is a hypothetical known-SD comparison; our actual five results do not tell us the true SD. The next slide replaces only that denominator by an estimate. The data-generating model has not changed, but the statistic and its sampling law have."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-normal-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Known SD: the standardized sample mean is normal"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-normal-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-normal-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-normal-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16c-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-normal-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-known ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-normal-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16c-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-estimated a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-t-estimated-h, ex:rev-t-estimated-v, ex:rev-t-estimated-c, ex:rev-t-estimated-f ; cd:narration "Replace the true individual SD by random sample SD. The standardized sample mean now divides by an estimated SE, not a fixed known number. Under independent normal sampling, mean and sample variance are independent. This ratio has Student-t law with n minus one degrees of freedom. For five repeats that is four. Small estimated denominators occasionally produce more extreme ratios, explaining the heavier tails relative to standard normal. This is the connection from our data to the plotted distribution, not a claim inferred from the shape of five values. Our observed mean is 10.0 and sample SD root 0.10; an unknown population mean remains in the inference formula. Next compare increasing df, then invert this statistic to report the mean interval."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-estimated-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Estimated SD: the standardized mean follows Student-t"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-estimated-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-estimated-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-estimated-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16d-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-estimated-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-est ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-estimated-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16d-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-convergence a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-t-convergence-h, ex:rev-t-convergence-c, ex:rev-t-convergence-v ; cd:narration "Compare t with four, ten and thirty degrees of freedom against standard normal on common axes. Four df comes from our five normal repeats. Ten and thirty would correspond to eleven and thirty-one results when one mean is estimated. As normal sample size increases, sample SD estimates true SD more stably. The estimated denominator approaches the known one and Student-t converges to standard normal. No finite df gives exact equality, and visual overlap in the centre does not guarantee negligible tail differences for every probability. This is not evidence that arbitrary data become normal at sample size thirty."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-convergence-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "More degrees of freedom bring Student-t closer to normal"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-convergence-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-convergence-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-convergence-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-limit ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-convergence-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-convergence-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-report a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-t-report-h, ex:rev-t-report-c, ex:rev-t-report-f ; cd:narration "Return to the same five constructed nitrate repeats. Their mean is 10.0 mg/L, sample variance 0.10 squared mg/L and estimated SE root 0.10 divided by root five, or 0.141421 mg/L. The t-four quantile at cumulative probability 0.975 is about 2.776445, larger than the known-SD normal quantile 1.959964. Multiply by estimated SE for margin 0.392649 mg/L. The two-sided 95 percent confidence interval for the common analytical expectation is 9.60735 to 10.39265 mg/L. Under the normal iid assumptions this procedure has 95 percent repeated-sampling coverage. It is not a 95 percent range of individual results, an environmental mean interval or total measurement uncertainty. The larger t multiplier is the practical consequence of estimating SD from just five results."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-report-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Use Student-t to report our five-result mean"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-report-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-report-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-report-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-t-ci ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-t-report-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-t16g-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:rev-f-process a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-f-distribution ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-f ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-f-process-h, ex:rev-f-process-v ; cd:narration "Begin with two independent method studies, not paired measurements. Each method gives six results on homogeneous control material, fits its own mean and estimates variance with five residual degrees of freedom. Prespecify method B variance divided by method A variance and the question whether B is less precise. Under equal true variances, the unknown common variance cancels from that ratio. Two independent scaled chi-square variance estimates then give the F reference with ordered five and five df. We next display actual constructed six-result series, obtain their variances and compare the ratio with the appropriate cutoff. F tests precision, not agreement of the means or accuracy against a reference value."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-process-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "F process: compare variances from two independent methods"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-process-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-f-process-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-process-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-f-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-story a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-f-distribution ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-f ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-f-story-h, ex:rev-f-story-c ; cd:narration "These new series are distinct from the previous five-repeat study. Method A's six values have mean 10.0, sum of squared deviations 0.20 and variance 0.04. Method B has mean 10.0, sum 0.80 and variance 0.16, all in the appropriate concentration units. Dividing each squared sum by five uses the one fitted mean in that series. Their variance ratio B over A is four. The symmetric teaching values were constructed to make arithmetic transparent; they do not prove normality or independence, and the two methods are not a paired design. The question and numerator order were fixed in advance. Equal observed means do not make this an accuracy study."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-story-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Our two methods: is B less precise than A"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-story-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-f-story-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-story-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-f-data ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-reference a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-f-distribution ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-f ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-f-reference-h, ex:rev-f-reference-v, ex:rev-f-reference-c, ex:rev-f-reference-f ; cd:narration "Connect the displayed data to the reference. Under independent normal sampling each sample variance is true variance times a chi-square divided by its residual df. If true variances are equal, that common factor cancels in B over A. The ratio therefore has F law with numerator five and denominator five df. Without equality, the raw variance ratio has an additional unknown scale factor. The family curves are theoretical references, not particle-size or concentration distributions. The next slide compares our observed ratio four with the actual F-five-five critical value, preserving the predeclared one-sided question."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-reference-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Equal true variances give our ratio an F reference"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-reference-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-f-reference-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-reference-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-f17b-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-reference-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-f-context ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-reference-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-f17b-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-decision a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-f-distribution ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-f ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:rev-f-decision-h, ex:rev-f-decision-c, ex:rev-f-decision-f ; cd:narration "Our ratio 0.16 over 0.04 is four. Under equal true variances the one-sided five-percent upper cutoff for F with five and five df is 5.050329. Four is below this value, so do not reject equal variances against the predeclared larger-B alternative at five percent. A fourfold observed ratio can still be compatible with substantial sampling variation from two small series. Non-rejection does not establish equal precision, normality or method accuracy. Reversing the ratio after viewing the results would require a different decision rule."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-decision-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "F decision: 4 is below the 5% cutoff"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-decision-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-f-decision-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-decision-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:rev-f-result ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:rev-f-decision-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-f-work ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
}

<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/knowledge/level5-storyline> {
  ex:rev-t-convergence-chart a cd:ChartDefinition ; skos:prefLabel "Normal and Student-t with 4, 10 and 30 df"@en ; cd:body "Normal is the solid reference. Student-t curves approach it as df increases; finite df retains heavier tails. Same axes and no density standardization to unit variance."@en ; cd:chartType cd:LineChart ; cd:chartPresentation "complete" ; cd:showChartPoints false ; cd:chartYMinimum 0.0 ; cd:chartYMaximum 0.42 ; cd:usesDataset ex:l5-c14-standard-normal ; dct:hasPart ex:l5-t16-t4, ex:l5-t16-t10, ex:l5-t16-t30 ; cd:xAxisLabel "Standardized sample-mean statistic"@en ; cd:xAxisUnit "1" ; cd:yAxisLabel "Density"@en ; cd:yAxisUnit "1" ; cd:authoredResource true .
